Toward a Primitive Theory of Cognitive Value

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta, Kimi 

Abstract

Modern artificial intelligence relies on a variety of objective functions—reward, information gain, empowerment, free energy—each tied to a specific class of algorithms and assuming a pre‑existing repertoire of cognitive operations. No generally accepted theory explains why a cognitive operation should be instantiated at all before any task‑specific algorithm is chosen. This paper proposes Cognitive Primitive Value (CPV), a scalar quantity grounded in a resource‑theoretic analysis of cognitive conversions. We show that under mild axioms—additivity, continuity, and monotonicity—any monotone quantifying the feasibility of converting predictive accuracy, controllability, and computational effort must take the form of a linear combination of evidence gain, empowerment gain, and complexity cost. Defining CPV as exactly this combination yields a unifying normative objective. Three central results follow: the Projection Theorem proves that under appropriate constraints CPV reduces to the standard objectives of expected information gain, reward‑seeking, empowerment, and (a variant) expected free energy; the Suppression Theorem gives a precise condition under which a cognitive operation is never realized; and the Asymptotic Optimality Theorem demonstrates that a CPV‑maximizing agent, in the limit of unbounded resources, performs Solomonoff induction and, when augmented with a reward‑biased operation, attains the AIXI‑level universal optimality up to an additive constant. These results establish CPV as a candidate unifying objective for memory, reasoning, planning, and perception, and provide a common theoretical foundation for resource‑rational autonomous intelligence.

---

1. Introduction

Intelligent systems constantly select and execute cognitive operations: attending to a stimulus, retrieving a memory, mentally simulating a plan, or refining a hypothesis. The objectives that have been proposed to guide this selection are legion—maximizing expected cumulative reward (Sutton & Barto, 2018), minimizing variational free energy (Friston, 2010), increasing mutual information between actions and future states (Klyubin et al., 2005), or accelerating compression progress (Schmidhuber, 2010). Each framework has achieved remarkable success in its intended domain, yet every one starts by presupposing a fixed space of cognitive operations—actions, policies, messages, memory writes—and an evaluative structure defined over that space. None asks the prior question: why does that operation space exist at all, and why are some possible operations never instantiated?

This paper investigates a deeper layer: is there a single scalar whose maximization governs the very existence, selection, and suppression of every cognitive operation, before a specific task or algorithm is chosen? We argue that such a scalar should not be a hand‑picked sum of desirable terms but should arise from a principled theory of cognitive resource convertibility. Drawing on the mathematical framework of general resource theories (Coecke, Fritz & Spekkens, 2016), we treat a cognitive state as a bundle of resources—predictive accuracy, controllability (empowerment), and a bounded computational budget. A cognitive operation is a transformation of this bundle, and its feasibility is determined by whether it yields a non‑negative net increase in a suitable cognitive value. From a small set of axioms (additivity, continuity, monotonicity) we deduce that the only quantitative measure of net resource change consistent with a preorder on cognitive states is a linear combination of evidence gain, empowerment gain, and minus a complexity cost. We call this measure Cognitive Primitive Value (CPV).

The resulting framework makes no arbitrary choices: the evidence gain is the expected increase in log predictive probability under the agent’s current model—exactly the expected information gain in Bayesian experimental design (Lindley, 1956). The empowerment gain measures the change in the agent’s channel capacity to influence future sensor states, computed entirely within its own world‑model. The complexity cost is the conditional Kolmogorov complexity of the operation given the current state, weighted by a resource coefficient that reflects the tightness of the computational budget. CPV is then the net change in cognitive value.

We prove three central theorems. The Projection Theorem shows that when the operation is restricted to a Bayesian update, a reward‑biased update, or a pure control change, CPV reduces exactly to expected information gain, expected reward minus cost, or empowerment gain, respectively. A variant of CPV that replaces empowerment with a preference term yields the expected free energy of active inference. The Suppression Theorem states that any operation that leaves both predictive power and controllability unchanged is never realized because its CPV is strictly negative for any positive complexity. The Asymptotic Optimality Theorem demonstrates that as computational resources grow unbounded, a CPV‑maximizing agent learns the true environment distribution (Solomonoff induction) and, when allowed to use reward‑biased operations, achieves a cumulative reward that approaches the AIXI universal optimum up to a constant. Together, these results position CPV as a strong candidate for the unified objective of resource‑rational cognition.

