3. Formal Definitions

3.1 Evidence Gain

For an operation that replaces the model M by M', the evidence gain is

\Delta \mathcal{E}(c \mid z) \;=\; P(z') - P(z)
= \mathbb{E}_{o \sim p(\cdot \mid M)}\Bigl[\, \ell(o; M') - \ell(o; M) \Bigr],

where \ell(o; M) = \mathbb{E}_{\theta \sim M}[\log p(o \mid \theta)] is the expected log‑likelihood of observation o under the model’s own parameter distribution. The expectation over o is taken under the old model’s predictive distribution p(\cdot \mid M). This is exactly the expected improvement in predictive accuracy as assessed by the model itself.

3.2 Empowerment Gain

Empowerment is defined internally for a model M as

\mathfrak{E}_M = \max_{p(a_{0:n-1})} I_M(A_{0:n-1}; S_n),

where n is the planning horizon, a fixed parameter of the cognitive architecture. The maximization is over open‑loop action distributions; the mutual information is computed inside the agent’s generative model M. The empowerment gain of an operation is \Delta \mathfrak{E} = \mathfrak{E}_{M'} - \mathfrak{E}_M.

3.3 Cognitive Primitive Value

Combining the components, the CPV of an operation c at state z = (M, h, B) is

\boxed{
\mathrm{CPV}(c \mid z) \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{o \sim p(\cdot \mid M)}\Bigl[ \ell(o; M') - \ell(o; M) \Bigr]
\;+\; \bigl( \mathfrak{E}_{M'} - \mathfrak{E}_M \bigr)
\;-\; \frac{1}{B}\,K(c \mid z).
}

A cognitive path \gamma = (c_1, \dots, c_k) has cumulative CPV \Phi(\gamma) = \sum_{t=1}^k \mathrm{CPV}(c_t \mid z_{t-1}), where z_t = c_t(z_{t-1}).

Principle of Cognitive Convertibility.
A path \gamma is realizable iff \Phi(\gamma) \ge 0. Among realizable paths, the agent selects one with probability proportional to \exp(\beta \Phi(\gamma)), where \beta > 0 is an inverse temperature. In the optimality limit \beta \to \infty, the agent chooses a path that maximizes \Phi(\gamma).

