4. Projection Theorem

Theorem 1 (Projection). Under specific restrictions on the operation, CPV simplifies to well‑known cognitive objectives.

(a) Pure Bayesian update (expected information gain).
Suppose c is an operation that, for a contemplated observation o', replaces M by the Bayesian posterior M_{o'} and leaves empowerment unchanged (\Delta \mathfrak{E} = 0). Then

\mathrm{CPV}(c \mid z) = I(\Theta; O' \mid M) - \lambda K(c \mid z),

where I(\Theta; O' \mid M) is the mutual information between model parameters and the next observation. In particular, if K(c \mid z) is constant across such inference steps, maximizing CPV over possible o' is equivalent to maximizing expected information gain.

Proof. By definition of the Bayesian update,

\ell(o'; M_{o'}) = \mathbb{E}_{\theta \sim M_{o'}}[\log p(o' \mid \theta)] = \int \log p(o' \mid \theta)\, p(\theta \mid o', M) d\theta.

The evidence gain becomes

\Delta\mathcal{E} = \mathbb{E}_{o' \sim p(\cdot \mid M)}\bigl[ \mathbb{E}_{\theta \mid o'}[\log p(o' \mid \theta)] - \log p(o' \mid M) \bigr].

This is exactly the standard expression for the mutual information I(\Theta; O' \mid M) (Lindley 1956). With \Delta\mathfrak{E} = 0, the result follows. \square

(b) Reward‑biased update (bounded‑rational RL).
Assume the operation produces a model M' whose predictive distribution is exponentially tilted toward high reward:

p(o' \mid M') \propto p(o' \mid M) \, \exp(\eta R(o')), \qquad \eta > 0,

and that empowerment is unchanged. Then

\mathrm{CPV}(c \mid z) = \eta\,\mathbb{E}_{o' \sim p(\cdot \mid M)}[R(o')] - \log Z - \lambda K(c \mid z),

where Z = \mathbb{E}_{p(\cdot \mid M)}[\exp(\eta R(o'))]. Maximizing CPV over sequences yields a sum of expected rewards minus complexity costs, i.e., bounded‑rational reinforcement learning.

Proof. For the tilted model, \ell(o'; M') = \log p(o' \mid M') because the tilt is applied directly to the predictive distribution. Hence

\log p(o' \mid M') = \eta R(o') - \log Z + \log p(o' \mid M).

Substituting into the evidence gain gives \Delta\mathcal{E} = \mathbb{E}_{o'}[\eta R(o') - \log Z]. With \Delta\mathfrak{E} = 0, the CPV expression follows. \square

(c) Pure empowerment operation.
If the operation changes only the action repertoire, leaving the predictive distribution unchanged (so \ell(o; M') = \ell(o; M) for all o), then \Delta\mathcal{E} = 0 and

\mathrm{CPV}(c \mid z) = (\mathfrak{E}_{M'} - \mathfrak{E}_M) - \lambda K(c \mid z).

(d) Relation to active inference.
If we replace the empowerment term in CPV by a preference term \mathbb{E}_{o \sim p(\cdot \mid M)}[\log \tilde{p}(o)], where \tilde{p} is a prior over desired observations, then CPV becomes exactly the negative expected free energy -\mathcal{G}(\pi) of active inference (Friston et al. 2015), minus the computational cost. This variant separates the epistemic (evidence gain) and pragmatic (preference) drives in the manner of active inference, showing that CPV subsumes that framework as a special projection.

