5. Suppression Theorem

Theorem 2 (Suppression).
If an operation c leaves both the evidence and the empowerment unchanged, i.e.

\ell(o; M') = \ell(o; M) \quad \forall o, \qquad \mathfrak{E}_{M'} = \mathfrak{E}_M,

then \mathrm{CPV}(c \mid z) = -\lambda K(c \mid z) \le 0. Moreover, if K(c \mid z) > 0, the value is strictly negative. By the Convertibility Principle, any path consisting solely of such an operation has negative cumulative CPV and is therefore never realized.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. \square

Corollary 2.1 (Selective forgetting).
A memory‑formation operation that improves neither prediction nor control is automatically suppressed. This provides a normative explanation for adaptive forgetting and the scarcity of conscious cognitive operations.

