6. Asymptotic Optimality Theorem

We now consider the behavior of a CPV‑maximizing agent as the computational budget grows without bound, \lambda = 1/B \to 0, in the optimal choice limit \beta \to \infty. We assume the true environment \mu is a computable conditional semimeasure.

Theorem 3 (Asymptotic Optimality).

1. Learning convergence. If the agent uses only pure Bayesian update operations, its model M_t converges to the true environment \mu in the sense that
   \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \mathbb{E}_\mu\Bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(\mu(\cdot \mid h_{<t}) \,\|\, M_t(\cdot \mid h_{<t})\bigr) \Bigr] \le K(\mu) \ln 2 + O(1),
   almost surely. Hence the agent performs Solomonoff induction.
2. Reward optimality. If, in addition, the operation repertoire contains the reward‑biased update of Theorem 1(b) with temperature \eta > 0, the CPV‑maximizing policy \pi_{\mathrm{CPV}} satisfies, for every computable \mu,
   V_\mu^{\pi_{\mathrm{CPV}}} \;\ge\; V_\mu^{\pi_{\mathrm{AIXI}}} - O(K(\mu)),
   where V_\mu^\pi is the expected sum of discounted rewards and \pi_{\mathrm{AIXI}} is the AIXI policy.

Proof sketch.

(1) Pure inference. As B \to \infty, the cost term vanishes. For pure Bayesian updates, empowerment is unchanged, so CPV reduces to the evidence gain \Delta\mathcal{E} = I(\Theta; O' \mid M). The agent therefore chooses at each step an observation (or an internal simulation thereof) that maximizes expected information gain. The cumulative information after t steps is

I(\Theta; h_{<t}) = \sum_{\tau=1}^{t} I(\Theta; O_\tau \mid h_{<\tau}).

The function f(S) = I(\Theta; S) is known to be adaptive submodular (Krause & Guestrin 2005). For unbounded budget, the agent can simulate arbitrarily long sequences and effectively perform an exhaustive search, making the greedy policy optimal for cumulative mutual information. Using the Solomonoff prior w_\nu = 2^{-K(\nu)}, the total mutual information is bounded by the expected log‑ratio of the mixture \xi:

I(\Theta; h_{<t}) \le \mathbb{E}_\mu\!\bigl[-\log w_\mu\bigr] + O(1) = K(\mu)\ln 2 + O(1).

Hutter’s (2005) Theorem 5.2.1 then implies the stated KL divergence bound, establishing that M_t converges to \mu.

(2) Adding reward. With the reward‑biased operation available, the agent can interleave information‑gathering and reward‑seeking steps. In the B\to\infty limit, the cumulative CPV of a path becomes

\sum \Bigl( I(\Theta; O_t \mid h_{<t}) + \eta R_t - \log Z_t \Bigr) + \text{(empowerment gains)},

where the empowerment terms add a bounded bonus because empowerment is capped by the finite horizon n. Since all costs vanish, the agent can choose at each point a sequence of mental operations that maximizes this sum, which is exactly the value of a Bayes‑adaptive MDP with an augmented reward signal \tilde R = \eta R + \text{info‑gain}. The Bayes‑optimal policy for this augmented MDP under the Solomonoff prior is the AIXI policy (Hutter 2005, Sec. 5.3). The additional information‑gain term contributes at most O(K(\mu)) to the total value, so the difference between V_\mu^{\pi_{\mathrm{CPV}}} and the true AIXI optimum is bounded by a constant depending on K(\mu). \square

Thus, in the unbounded‑resource limit, CPV maximization recovers both universal Bayesian learning and near‑universal optimal sequential decision making, unifying them within a single normative principle.

