7. Discussion

We have presented Cognitive Primitive Value as a unifying objective derived from a resource‑theoretic analysis of cognitive convertibility. Its form—a linear combination of evidence gain, empowerment gain, and a complexity cost—is not assumed but forced by mild additivity, continuity, and monotonicity axioms. The Projection Theorem demonstrates that every major proposed cognitive drive (information gain, reward maximization, empowerment, and, with a variant, expected free energy) arises as a special case. The Suppression Theorem gives a principled existence condition that predicts selective memory and attentional filtering. The Asymptotic Optimality Theorem shows that the ideal CPV‑maximizing agent learns the true environment and, when augmented with a reward bias, matches the universal performance of AIXI up to a constant.

Several design choices merit explicit discussion. The reward‑seeking drive enters the core theory only through a separate biased operation; CPV itself is intrinsically a combination of prediction and control. This is analogous to active inference, which requires a preference prior \tilde{p}(o) to generate goal‑directed behavior. We leave open whether the evidence‑gain term alone, when observations include naturally fitness‑encoding signals, can generate reward‑seeking without an explicit bias. The Kolmogorov complexity term carries a universal‑machine dependence that renders CPV values ambiguous by an additive constant; this constant is harmless in all asymptotic and suppression results, where the sign or relative magnitude is what matters.

The present derivation relies on the assumption that achievable resource‑change vectors span a continuum and that \Phi is continuous. In a finite, discrete cognitive architecture, the additivity and monotonicity constraints may still force linearity on average, but a fully discrete characterization is an open problem. Moreover, the empowerment horizon n and the inverse temperature \beta remain free parameters that can be tuned to match empirical data or hardware constraints.

In conclusion, Cognitive Primitive Value offers a mathematically principled, unifying answer to the question of why cognitive operations exist. By grounding the selection of operations in the same resource‑theoretic logic that governs thermodynamics and information processing, it bridges the gap between the normative ideals of universal intelligence and the resource‑rational reality of finite minds. The present work lays a rigorous foundation upon which both theoretical extensions and practical implementations can be built.

