A. Axiomatic Derivation of Cognitive Primitive Value

A.1 Resource vectors and convertibility
Let \mathcal{Z} be the set of cognitive states. Each state z carries a resource vector

\mathbf{R}(z)=\bigl(P(z), C(z), B(z)\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^3,
\]  

where P(z) and C(z) are the predictive and control resources defined in §2.1, and B(z) is the computational budget.
A cognitive operation is a partial computable function c: z\mapsto z'. Its net resource change is

\mathbf{v}(c)=\bigl(\Delta P,\Delta C,-K(c\mid z)\bigr),\qquad 
\Delta P=P(z')-P(z),\;\;\Delta C=C(z')-C(z).
\]  

(The minus sign on the complexity cost indicates that larger complexity consumes resources.)

A path \gamma=(c_1,\dots,c_k) transforms z_0 into z_k; its resource‑change vector is the sum \mathbf{v}(\gamma)=\sum_{t=1}^k\mathbf{v}(c_t).

A.2 Axioms for a cognitive value functional
We seek a real‑valued functional \Phi on paths such that a path is realisable iff \Phi(\gamma)\ge 0. \Phi must satisfy:

1. Additivity. For any two paths \gamma_1,\gamma_2 that can be composed,
   \Phi(\gamma_1\circ\gamma_2)=\Phi(\gamma_1)+\Phi(\gamma_2).
2. Continuity. \Phi depends continuously on the resource‑change vector; i.e., if \mathbf{v}^{(m)}\to\mathbf{v} in \mathbb{R}^3 then \Phi(\gamma^{(m)})\to\Phi(\gamma) whenever the resource‑change vectors of \gamma^{(m)} converge to that of \gamma.
3. Monotonicity. If an operation c has zero complexity cost (K(c\mid z)=0) and does not decrease predictive or control resource (\Delta P\ge0,\Delta C\ge0), then \Phi(c)\ge0.

A.3 Representation theorem
Because the value of a single operation can depend only on its resource‑change vector, we write \Phi(c)=f(\mathbf{v}(c)) for some function f:\mathcal{V}\to\mathbb{R}, where \mathcal{V}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^3 is the set of all achievable net‑change vectors. For two consecutive operations we have

f(\mathbf{v}_1+\mathbf{v}_2)=\Phi(c_1\circ c_2)=\Phi(c_1)+\Phi(c_2)=f(\mathbf{v}_1)+f(\mathbf{v}_2).
\]  

Thus f is additive on \mathcal{V}. We assume \mathcal{V} spans \mathbb{R}^3; if not, the argument is restricted to its linear span. A function additive on \mathbb{R}^3 and continuous at a single point is necessarily linear (this is the Cauchy functional equation; see Aczél (1966)). Hence there exist constants \alpha,\beta,\gamma\in\mathbb{R} with

\Phi(c)=\alpha\Delta P+\beta\Delta C-\gamma K(c\mid z). \tag{A.1}

The negative sign before \gamma is chosen for convenience; monotonicity forces \alpha,\beta\ge0 and \gamma\ge0.

We may rescale the units of P and C to set \alpha=\beta=1. The remaining coefficient \gamma is reinterpreted as the reciprocal of the momentary budget, \gamma=\lambda=1/B, which reflects that the same absolute cost of a computation is felt more severely when the budget is small. Thus

\Phi(c)=\Delta P+\Delta C-\frac{1}{B}K(c\mid z) \equiv \mathrm{CPV}(c\mid z).

A.4 Uniqueness
Any other additive, continuous monotone G satisfying the same axioms must also be linear with non‑negative coefficients for the resource gains and a non‑positive coefficient for the cost. Hence G=\kappa\,\Phi for some \kappa>0. Normalising the relative weight of prediction and control to unity eliminates the scaling ambiguity, leaving only the choice of \lambda=1/B. Thus CPV is the unique (up to the budget‑determined coefficient) continuous additive monotone for cognitive convertibility.

