B. Information‑Theoretic Identities

B.1 Refined evidence gain
For a model M with parameter distribution p(\theta\mid M) and likelihood p(o\mid\theta), the expected log‑likelihood of an observation o under M is

\ell(o;M)=\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim M}[\log p(o\mid\theta)].

The evidence gain of an operation c that changes the model from M to M' is

\Delta\mathcal{E}(c\mid z)=\mathbb{E}_{o\sim p(\cdot\mid M)}[\ell(o;M')-\ell(o;M)].

B.2 Bayesian update and mutual information
Let c_{\text{Bayes}} be the operation that, for a contemplated observation o', replaces M by the posterior M_{o'} defined by Bayes’ rule. Then

\ell(o';M_{o'}) = \mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim M_{o'}}[\log p(o'\mid\theta)].

The prior predictive density is p(o'\mid M)=\int p(o'\mid\theta)p(\theta\mid M)d\theta.

Lemma B.1.

\Delta\mathcal{E}(c_{\text{Bayes}}\mid z)=I(\Theta;O'\mid M),
\]  

the mutual information between the parameter \Theta and the next observation O'.

Proof.

\Delta\mathcal{E} = \mathbb{E}_{o'\sim p(\cdot\mid M)}\bigl[ \mathbb{E}_{\theta\mid o'}[\log p(o'\mid\theta)] - \log p(o'\mid M) \bigr]
= \mathbb{E}_{o'}\!\left[\int \log\frac{p(o'\mid\theta)}{p(o'\mid M)}p(\theta\mid o',M)d\theta\right].
\]  

By Bayes’ rule, p(\theta\mid o',M)=p(o'\mid\theta)p(\theta\mid M)/p(o'\mid M). Substituting and rearranging yields exactly the KL divergence D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p(\theta\mid o',M)\| p(\theta\mid M)) averaged over o', which is the mutual information. \square

B.3 Reward‑biased model
Suppose the operation produces a model M' whose predictive distribution is

p(o'\mid M')=\frac{p(o'\mid M)\exp(\eta R(o'))}{\mathbb{E}_{p(\cdot\mid M)}[\exp(\eta R(O))]},
\]  

with \eta>0 and reward function R. Then

\ell(o';M') = \log p(o'\mid M') = \eta R(o') - \log Z + \log p(o'\mid M),
\]  

where Z=\mathbb{E}_{p(\cdot\mid M)}[\exp(\eta R(O))]. Hence

\Delta\mathcal{E} = \mathbb{E}_{o'\sim p(\cdot\mid M)}[\eta R(o')-\log Z].

B.4 Pure empowerment case
If \ell(o;M')=\ell(o;M) for all o, then \Delta\mathcal{E}=0 by definition.

