C. Proof of the Projection Theorem

The Projection Theorem (Theorem 1) follows immediately from the identities in Appendix B and the definition of CPV. We restate each part with its explicit conditions.

Theorem 1(a). If c is the Bayesian update operation and \Delta\mathfrak{E}=0, then

\mathrm{CPV}(c\mid z)=I(\Theta;O'\mid M)-\lambda K(c\mid z).

Proof. Insert \Delta\mathcal{E}=I(\Theta;O'\mid M) (Lemma B.1) and \Delta\mathfrak{E}=0 into the CPV formula. \square

Theorem 1(b). If c is the reward‑biased update with tilt \eta and \Delta\mathfrak{E}=0, then

\mathrm{CPV}(c\mid z) = \eta\,\mathbb{E}[R(O')] - \log Z - \lambda K(c\mid z).

Proof. Insert the expression for \Delta\mathcal{E} from §B.3. \square

Theorem 1(c). If \Delta\mathcal{E}=0, then

\mathrm{CPV}(c\mid z) = (\mathfrak{E}_{M'}-\mathfrak{E}_{M}) - \lambda K(c\mid z).

Theorem 1(d). If we replace \Delta\mathfrak{E} in the CPV definition by a preference term \mathbb{E}_{o\sim p(\cdot\mid M)}[\log\tilde{p}(o)], the resulting quantity equals the negative expected free energy -\mathcal{G}(\pi) of active inference (Friston et al. 2015) minus the computational cost. The proof is a direct substitution; see Appendix G.

