D. Proof of the Suppression Theorem

Theorem 2. If \ell(o;M')=\ell(o;M) for all o and \mathfrak{E}_{M'}=\mathfrak{E}_{M}, then

\mathrm{CPV}(c\mid z) = -\lambda K(c\mid z)\le 0,
\]  

with strict inequality when K(c\mid z)>0.

Proof. Under the hypotheses, both the evidence gain integral and the empowerment difference vanish. The definition of CPV leaves only the negative complexity cost. Because \lambda=1/B>0 and K(c\mid z)\ge0, the value is non‑positive, and strictly negative when the complexity is positive. \square

