E. Proof of the Asymptotic Optimality Theorem

E.1 Learning convergence
Assume the agent uses only pure Bayesian update operations, the budget B\to\infty (so \lambda\to0), and the choice rule selects the maximally informative path (\beta\to\infty). Then at each step the agent chooses an operation that maximises evidence gain, which by Theorem 1(a) is exactly the expected information gain I(\Theta; O' \mid M).

The sequence of observations (real or simulated) is chosen greedily to maximise mutual information. The function S\mapsto I(\Theta; S) is adaptive submodular (Krause & Guestrin 2005). For unbounded horizon, the greedy policy achieves the maximum possible cumulative mutual information up to a constant factor (1-1/e); however, because the agent can simulate arbitrarily long sequences without real‑world cost, it can perform an exhaustive search, making the greedy policy effectively optimal in the limit.

Let \xi be the Solomonoff mixture over all lower semicomputable semimeasures \nu with prior w_\nu=2^{-K(\nu)}. The agent’s initial model is \xi (or a computable approximation thereof). Hutter (2005, Thm. 5.2.1) shows that for any computable true environment \mu,

\sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \mathbb{E}_\mu\Bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(\mu(\cdot\mid h_{<t})\,\|\,\xi(\cdot\mid h_{<t})\bigr) \Bigr] \le K(\mu)\ln 2.

Consequently, the predictive distribution of the mixture converges to \mu almost surely. Since the agent’s model M_t is updated by Bayes’ rule from the mixture, it inherits this convergence. This establishes Theorem 3(1).

E.2 Reward optimality
Now let the operation repertoire include the reward‑biased update of Theorem 1(b). With B\to\infty, the cost term vanishes. The agent can freely interleave information‑gathering and reward‑seeking steps. The cumulative CPV of a path becomes

\sum \bigl( I(\Theta; O_t\mid h_{<t}) + \eta R(o_t) - \log Z_t \bigr) + \text{(empowerment gains)}.

Because empowerment is bounded (by the finite horizon n), its contribution is at most a constant per step; in the long horizon it does not dominate. The agent chooses the sequence of mental and external actions that maximises this sum. This is equivalent to solving a Bayes‑adaptive Markov decision process (BAMDP) with an augmented reward signal

\tilde{R}_t = \eta R(o_t) + I(\Theta; O_t\mid h_{<t}) - \log Z_t.

When the prior over environments is the Solomonoff prior, the Bayes‑optimal policy for this BAMDP is exactly the AIXI policy (Hutter 2005, Sec. 5.3). The additional information‑gain term contributes at most O(K(\mu)) to the total value, because the total mutual information between the environment and the infinite history is bounded by K(\mu)\ln 2. Hence the difference between the value of the CPV‑maximising policy and the AIXI optimum is bounded by a constant depending only on K(\mu). This proves Theorem 3(2). \square

