F. Empowerment: Computation and Bounds

F.1 Definition and properties
Empowerment is the channel capacity between the agent’s action sequence A_{0:n-1} and the future sensor state S_n under model M:

\mathfrak{E}_M = \max_{p(a_{0:n-1})} I_M(A_{0:n-1}; S_n).

It is non‑negative and equals zero iff S_n is independent of the action sequence. Because the mutual information is evaluated inside the agent’s own model, empowerment is an internal control resource.

F.2 Computation via Blahut–Arimoto
The maximisation is a convex problem that can be solved by the iterative Blahut–Arimoto algorithm (Blahut 1972). The update rule is

p^{(k+1)}(a) \propto p^{(k)}(a) \exp\!\Bigl( D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(p(s_n\mid a,M)\,\|\,p^{(k)}(s_n)\bigr) \Bigr),
\]  

which converges monotonically to the channel capacity.

F.3 Horizon scaling
In the asymptotic theorem we let the empowerment horizon n grow with the budget, e.g., n = \lfloor\log B\rfloor. This ensures that the control resource remains relevant but does not become arbitrarily expensive to compute.

---

G. Relation to Expected Free Energy

G.1 Variant of CPV with preferences
Define a modified CPV by replacing the empowerment term with a preference term:

\mathrm{CPV}_{\text{pref}}(c\mid z) = \Delta\mathcal{E} + \mathbb{E}_{o\sim p(\cdot\mid M)}[\log \tilde{p}(o)] - \lambda K(c\mid z),
\]  

where \tilde{p}(o) is a prior over desired observations (the “goal prior”). Under a pure Bayesian update, the sum of the evidence gain and the preference term equals the negative expected free energy -\mathcal{G}(\pi) of active inference (Friston et al. 2015), because

-\mathcal{G}(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_{q(o)}\!\bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(s|o)\|p(s|o)) \bigr] - \mathbb{E}_{q(o)}[\log \tilde{p}(o)],
\]  

and the first term is exactly the expected information gain when the approximate posterior q equals the exact posterior. The correspondence is exact; the computational cost adds a resource‑rational penalty.

G.2 Why the core CPV omits preferences
The main CPV definition uses empowerment rather than a goal prior because empowerment requires no hand‑specified reward or preference, making CPV a fully intrinsic resource measure. The active‑inference variant demonstrates that CPV can be seamlessly extended to incorporate extrinsic goals.

---

H. Kolmogorov Complexity Conventions

All occurrences of K(x\mid y) refer to the conditional prefix Kolmogorov complexity with respect to a fixed universal Turing machine U. The values are therefore defined up to an additive constant O(1). This constant does not affect:

· Suppression: if K(c\mid z)>0 the value remains strictly negative for sufficiently large complexity.
· Asymptotic limits: the constant is absorbed into the O(1) terms that vanish relative to B\to\infty.
· Uniqueness: the representation theorem identifies the functional up to an additive constant that we set to zero by normalising \Phi=0 on the empty operation.

For practical implementation, K must be replaced by a computable upper bound from a compression algorithm; the theoretical results guarantee that such an approximation preserves the structural properties as long as the bound is tight.

