I. Additional Lemmas

I.1 Adaptive submodularity of information gain
Let \mathcal{T} be a set of potential observations. Define the set function f(S) = I(\Theta; S) for S\subseteq\mathcal{T}. Then f is adaptive submodular and monotone (Krause & Guestrin 2005, Sec. 5). This ensures that the greedy policy that at each step picks the observation maximising the expected conditional information gain obtains at least (1-1/e) of the optimal value. In the limit of unbounded computational budget, the agent can simulate all possible sequences, thereby closing the gap and achieving optimality.

I.2 Bound on empowerment contribution
Because the observation space is finite and the horizon n is fixed, empowerment \mathfrak{E}_M is bounded above by \min\{\log|\mathcal{O}|, n\log|\mathcal{A}|\}. Therefore, in any long sequence of operations, the total empowerment gain is O(T), which does not outweigh the rewards or information gains in the asymptotic reward‑optimality proof.

